loading that is mainly due to induced drag through the rotation of the lift vector by the inflow angle as per BET. The torque loading in Fig. 7 therefore shows a higher torque for the smaller kernel sizes up to 80% blade span. Toward the tip, the BVI described previously changes the shape of the loading compared to the reference B-R case and creates higher drag and torque. Because of the discrepancy created by the BVI that is not properly captured in the tip region, it becomes difficult to decipher which Gaussian smearing produces the best results in this region.
